{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cfru6oErbZqQVMXngqND4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The plan phase for 2026-10-09 is finished. The brief is in `journal/2026-10-09/20-brief.md`, and the plan is marked done in `state.json` as the last step.\n\n**What gets built:** `@one-a-day/community/ancestor-birthplace-chart/v1`, \"Ancestor Birthplace Chart\". It is a still image drawn for 1920x1080 that also has to work in portrait, square and the seven test canvases.\n- **Layout:** five equal columns, one per generation, with 1, 2, 4, 8 and 16 cells. Each person's cell sits level with their father above and their mother below. Every cell is coloured by where that person was born, a legend at the bottom gives each place and its count, and missing ancestors are grey.\n- **Text in the cells:** when a cell is too small, its text steps down column by column, from name, year and place to just the number, then to colour only. Text is never cut off with \"...\".\n- **Props (eight, plus the debug switch):** rows typed per person, or a pasted GEDCOM export, which replaces the rows. You can choose which root person to chart, which lets you make one chart per relative, and whether to use birth or death places. Places in the home country (USA by default) are coloured by state, all others by country. Any colour can be overridden.\n- **Default sample:** a made-up family, labelled as fictional on the chart. I checked its counts with a script: 28 of 31 people known, and the legend reads Ohio 4 / Pennsylvania 4 / Kentucky 4 / Ireland 4 / Germany 4 / Virginia 3 / Sweden 3 / New York 2 / Unknown 3.\n- **Variants:** b adds a bar under each generation showing its share of each place (one reader did this by hand in 2016). c copies the 2016 Excel look.\n\nTwo reviewer agents went over the draft before I marked it done. They found four rules the build checker can't express: an exact count of 31 cells, the parent-child cell alignment, chart-versus-band overlap, and a size tolerance that fails on the smallest canvas. The brief now has the build's tests check those directly. They also corrected my predictions of how much text fits at each canvas size and two variants that broke the scoring list. All of it is fixed in the brief.\n\nOne editing script of mine briefly duplicated half the brief. I repaired it and checked that the structure and the sample block are intact.\n\n**Weak spots, also stated in the brief:**\n- DNA Painter and FamilySearch already colour family trees by country, and the trend peaked in 2016.\n- A GEDCOM goes in as text inside a props file, not as a file path.\n- At the two smallest canvases, the outer generation shows little more than colour and a number.\n\nNothing was committed and no file outside today's journal folder was touched."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":508,"cache_read_input_tokens":128419,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":508},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"46f0c3f2-336c-4ef6-8157-cd9cb5b4cbf6","uuid":"5f2bc1e8-fdb3-453a-bbb0-b75f9c6c8dec","timestamp":"2026-10-09T15:27:56.405Z","request_id":"req_011Cfru6ns2NhfVydoVnFte6"}
